WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:05.290
The real meaning of the name Antarctica kind&nbsp;
of blew my mind. Okay, so the first part “Ant-”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:10.810
is sort of like “anti-” just means sort of&nbsp;
“opposite,” but “-arctica” means “bear.” Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:14.770
so Antarctica literally means like “opposite&nbsp;
of the bear.” Obviously, there's no bears in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:19.150
Antarctica, by the way. And no, it has nothing&nbsp;
to do with polar bears being in the Arctic or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:23.590
the north. It has to do with the stars. There's&nbsp;
this one constellation that most of us know as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:27.250
the Big Dipper, but civilizations, for a lot of&nbsp;
years looked up at the stars and saw this as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:33.010
bear. Ursa Major. Sailors and navigators always&nbsp;
use this constellation to find the North Star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:36.130
Polaris. The way I always thought&nbsp;
about it is like the “dipper” of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:40.450
the Big Dipper kind of points to Polaris.&nbsp;
That's north, okay? So for a lot of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.470
this Big Dipper that kind of looks like a bear&nbsp;
up in the sky is sort of synonymous with “north.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:49.510
And so that's how we get “Antarctica.” That's&nbsp;
the opposite of the bear. If the bear is north,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.830
then the opposite of the north bear is&nbsp;
the south. The South Pole. Antarctica.

